GitHub Actions has native OIDC token issuance built into the platform. Jobs can request a signed JWT from GitHub's OIDC provider when the workflow grants `id-token: write` permission. The token contains claims that identify what is running, including the repository, branch or tag reference, deployment environment, and workflow file.
...
1. Runner requests a JWT from GitHub's OIDC provider (`token.actions.githubusercontent.com`) — requires `id-token: write` declared on the job
2. GitHub issues a JWT containing claims for the repository, branch, environment, and workflow file
3. Runner sends the JWT to Vault's JWT auth endpoint
4. Vault fetches signing keys from GitHub's shared OIDC discovery URL and validates the token signature
5. Vault evaluates `bound_claims` — repository, environment, ref, or `job_workflow_ref` — against the configured role
6. Vault issues a short-lived token scoped to the policies attached to the matching role
...
The right auth method depends on where GitHub Actions runners execute. JWT/OIDC auth works for all runner types because GitHub issues an OIDC token for any job with `id-token: write` permission, regardless of the underlying infrastructure. However, when jobs run on infrastructure you control, additional auth methods become available that can provide stronger identity guarantees or remove the dependency on reaching GitHub's OIDC endpoint.
...
