Individual taxa and morphological groups of the Avalon Assemblage persisted into the later Ediacaran White Sea and Nama Assemblages (Gehling & Droser 2013, Laflamme et al. 2013), but these younger, “second wave” Assemblages of the Ediacara Biota include a wide range of new taxa ( Figure 1 ). These new taxa are characterized by a range of notable biological and ecological innovations, as recorded by pronounced increases in the diversity of body plans and extent of ecospace utilization; the appearance of these second wave taxa constitutes a radiation distinct from the bracketing radiations of the Avalon Assemblage and the Cambrian. Prominently, this radiation included the appearance of a suite of novel body plans. These body plans mark a major morphological diversification of the Ediacara Biota, involving dramatic increases in mobility (Gehling et al. 2005, Droser et al. 2006, Grazhdankin 2014); the appearance of undisputed bilaterians, such as burrowing organisms and putative stem-group mollusks (Fedonkin & Waggoner 1997, Seilacher 1999, Gehling et al. 2014); the appearance of the first skeletons (Germs 1972, Gehling & Rigby 1996, Clites et al. 2012); and the advent of active heterotrophy by multicellular organisms (Seilacher 1999, Ivantsov & Malakhovskaya 2002, Fedonkin 2003, Seilacher et al. 2005, Gehling et al. 2014). Moreover, this diversification in macrofauna body plans and ecologies was accompanied by a diversification in organically bound microbial substrates (microbial mats) that are recorded in the form of extensive, physically and biologically mediated sedimentary textures. Within this second wave, a notable reduction in diversity occurred with the appearance of the Nama Assemblage. Although it may reflect an episode or interval of elevated extinction, the di…

