Relative to its mass, the biosphere disproportionately impacts other components of the Earth system. It oxygenates the oceans and atmosphere, regulates global geochemical cycles, and influences rates and patterns of global change. Life leaves distinctive signals in the rock record, and few are more striking than those at the onset of the Phanerozoic—one of the most intensely studied intervals in the geologic record. The Cambrian Explosion—as famously revealed in the Burgess and Maotianshan shales—represents the geologically abrupt (~25 million years [m.y.]) appearance and diversification of nearly all major metazoan phyla. This proliferation of the roots of the animal phylogenetic tree and rapid expansion of morphological complexity was one of the more significant macroevolutionary events in life history and coincided with a variety of global-scale biotic and abiotic changes (Fig. 1; Briggs et al., 1992; Erwin, 2007)—some of which were brought about by metazoan activities, while others elicited a response by metazoans.

