The early diversification of Euarthropoda gave rise to the most species-rich animal phylum on Earth, encompassing chelicerates, myriapods, crustaceans, insects, and their relatives. Many of the defining attributes of this clade—including articulated appendages, a chitinous exoskeleton, compound eyes, and an anterior region composed of multiple functionally specialized segments—are already documented in early Cambrian fossil assemblages, including the Chengjiang biota of Yunnan, China, at ca. 518 Ma. Among these features, the organization of the head is especially informative because it integrates sensory structures, feeding appendages, and the hypostome into a coordinated anterior feeding apparatus.1 Resolving how these components became aligned across segments has therefore been central to reconstructing the origin of the euarthropod body plan.1,2,3

