# The Cambrian conundrum: early divergence and later ecological success in the early history of animals.

Erwin DH et al.

Science. 2011 Nov 25; 334(6059):1091-1097
...
This fine paper assembles evidence from various sources, from molecular biology to ecology to palaeontology, and provides a fresh look at the evolution of Metazoan diversity based on multiple approaches. The life of a species, inferred from the fossil record, starts with their first appearance in the rocks and ends with their exit from the fossil record. But we can find living fossils, which means that even if a species is presumed to have become extinct a long time ago (due to lack of fossil remains since a given geological period) it can show up with living representatives. This also means that the birth of lineages might be much earlier than the date of their first appearance in the fossil record. I must confess my admiration for palaeontologists, because they can draw information from body architectures that I could never interpret as being meaningful. I study the recent Medusozoa, and I am very careful when giving names to species because, for many of them, one must know the whole life cycle when saying something about them. Studying the ecology and the development of these animals, furthermore, is revealing many interesting aspects that might be relevant to articles like this one. Erwin et al., in fact, propose evolutionary trees that invariably see the cnidaria at the base of metazoan evolution, and they still treat them as diploblasts. In a paper that is co-authored by myself {1}, it is shown how the medusa stage is truly triploblastic, and that the subumbrellar cavity of the Hydromedusae derives from cavitation of a layer of tissue that originates between the ectoderm and the endoderm in…

