[3] HVAC System Performance in Nevada Desert Climate | Nevada HVAC Authority
URL: https://nevadahvacauthority.com/desert-climate-hvac-performance
Split-system central air conditioners dominate Nevada residential and light commercial installations. In these systems, a compressor-condenser unit located outdoors rejects heat to ambient air while an air handler and evaporator coil inside the structure absorb indoor heat. In Nevada's summer ambient temperatures — Las Vegas regularly records afternoon highs above 110°F — the outdoor condensing unit operates at a thermal penalty: the smaller the differential between refrigerant condensing temperature and ambient air temperature, the less efficiently the refrigerant cycle rejects heat.

The coefficient of performance (COP) and seasonal energy efficiency ratio (SEER2) ratings assigned to equipment are tested under standardized conditions defined by AHRI Standard 210/240, which uses 95°F as the standard outdoor test temperature. When ambient temperatures climb to 110°F–115°F, actual field efficiency may be 15%–25% below rated SEER2 values, a divergence that affects both energy cost projections and equipment sizing validity.

Ductwork performance is a second structural variable of primary importance. In Nevada, ducts frequently pass through unconditioned attic spaces where temperatures routinely reach 150°F–160°F. Conductive and leakage losses through attic duct runs can reduce system delivered capacity by 20%–30%, as documented in building science research conducted under DOE Building Technologies Office protocols. Nevada HVAC installation standards define duct sealing and insulation requirements that address this specific failure pathway.
