...
• What the numbers mean: MOP (Maximum Overcurrent Protection) is the largest breaker or fuse allowed for the outdoor unit. MCA (Minimum Circuit Ampacity) is used to size the wire. The breaker must never be larger than the MOP. 
• Don't assume one breaker fits all. At the same tonnage, the required breaker can vary by up to 25A between brands and series. Replacing one brand with another may mean changing the breaker, and sometimes the wire. 
• GE NS18H: Offered in 2, 3, 4 and 5 tons (NS18H24HA5, NS18H36HA5, NS18H60HA5). The NS18H60HA5 covers both 4 and 5 tons, and at those sizes it needs the largest circuit on this list (60A MOP). 
• Goodman GLZS4BA: The 3-ton unit (35A) needs a larger breaker than the 3.5-ton (30A). Goodman requires time-delay fuses or HACR-type breakers. 
• Carrier: Values are for the current GH5SAN5 (R-454B). Older GH5SAN4 (R-410A) units may differ. Rheem: Values are for single-phase. 3–5 ton RP14AY units are also offered in 3-phase 208/230V and 460V with lower ratings.
