[4] Definite and Substantial Reliance: Remedying Injustice Under Section 90
URL: https://ir.lawnet.fordham.edu/cgi/viewcontent.cgi?article=3530&context=flr
FORDHAM LAW REVIEW the Restatement defined promissory estoppel as follows: "A promise which the promisor should reasonably expect to induce action or for bearance of a definite and substantial character on the part of the promisee and which does induce such action or forbearance is binding if injustice can be avoided only by enforcement of the promise."6 Be cause of the weighty reputations of the original framers and their avowed endeavor to state the prevalent legal principles of the day, many courts accepted section 90 as the authoritative definition of promissory estoppel.7

Almost a half-century later, the second Restatement of Contracts 
offered a revised version of the doctrine.8 It specifically removed the 
requirement for reliance of a definite and substantial character from 
section 90.9 The section now reads: 
A promise which the promisor should reasonably expect to induce 
action or forbearance on the part of the promisee or a third person 
and which does induce such action or forbearance is binding if injus 
tice can be avoided only by enforcement of the promise. The rem 
edy granted for breach may be limited as justice requires.' 0 
The former requirement to induce action of a definite and substantial 
character was relegated to a comment under section 90 as a factor that 
courts might consider in determining the appropriate remedy for the 
promisee." 
This Note examines whether courts currently require reliance of a 
definite and substantial character for the enforcement of promises, 
and thus, whether the Restatement in its present form accurately re 
flects the state of the doctrine of promissory estoppel. Part I of this 
Note discusses the origins of promissory estoppel and its encapsula 
tion in section 90 of each of the two Restatements of Contracts.…

