1. The strength of the original arrangement A verbal agreement can still hold significance, but enforcing it is considerably more challenging. Courts and HR departments tend to prioritize evidence that demonstrates a clear mutual understanding, such as: • Emails confirming approval • Records of supervisor discussions • HR or payroll forms indicating a remote status • Any changes in compensation or job descriptions related to remote work If your spouse relied on the company’s approval to make significant life decisions, like relocating out of state, that reliance can be crucial. This situation often falls under “promissory estoppel,” which means she acted based on a company promise and experienced harm when the company changed its stance. While I aim to avoid legal jargon, the concept is that a broken promise causing financial harm can sometimes serve as grounds for a claim.

