[1] Understanding the Promissory Estoppel Statute of Limitations in Texas (2025-04-01T02:50:35.000Z)
URL: https://abogadosgold.com/statute/promissory-estoppel-statute-of-limitations-texas/
Promissory estoppel is a legal principle that allows a party to enforce a promise made to them, even if the promise is not supported by consideration. This means that if someone makes a promise to you and you reasonably rely on that promise to your detriment, the law may prevent the promisor from going back on their word.

In Texas, there is a statute of limitations that applies to claims based on promissory estoppel. A statute of limitations sets the timeframe within which a lawsuit must be filed after the cause of action arises. In Texas, the statute of limitations for promissory estoppel claims is typically four years.
...
Promissory estoppel is a legal doctrine that allows a party to enforce a promise even without consideration. In the context of the statute of limitations in Texas, there are four conditions where promissory estoppel can be applied:

- Clear and Definite Promise: To invoke promissory estoppel, there must be a clear and definite promise made by one party to another.
- Reasonable Reliance: The party seeking to enforce the promise must have reasonably relied on the promise to their detriment. This means that they changed their position or behavior because of the promise.
- Injustice Without Enforcement: It must be unjust not to enforce the promise. If the promise is not enforced, it would result in unfairness or harm to the party who relied on it.
- Equitable Remedy: Promissory estoppel is an equitable remedy, meaning that it is used to prevent injustice and ensure fairness between the parties involved. Courts will apply this doctrine when necessary to avoid an unfair outcome.
...
