Estoppel, in legal terms, is a doctrine that prevents a party from asserting a right or claim that contradicts their previous actions, conduct, or representations, when another party has justifiably relied on that conduct to their detriment. It's essentially a fairness principle designed to prevent someone from going back on their word (or actions) when it would cause harm to someone else. In Texas, estoppel is an affirmative defense, meaning the party raising it bears the burden of proving its elements.
